{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1540136-using-nodes-in-conspiracies","id":1540136,"title":"Using Nodes in Conspiracies","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Deviant: The Renegades"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-10T11:55:57-07:00","id":1540136,"title":"Using Nodes in Conspiracies","content":"So when I first ran Deviant, I tried to use the provided system for conspiracies with nodes, lynchpins, actions, etc. After about 5 sessions, I stopped using that part of the system because it felt like it wasn't able to provide the kind of narrative response to the player actions that I wanted. It felt like a lot of busy-work (almost like a wargame) when I could just have the antagonists respond in a suitable dramatic way to player actions and increase the tension based on the feel of the game, as I would in other systems.<br /> <br /> Do other Deviant STs use this part of the system? Did I just not give it enough time to work?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27450-alaskanemily","name":"AlaskanEmily","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-10T15:15:35-07:00","id":1540145,"title":"","content":"You may need to clarify what you mean by &quot;the kind of narrative response to the player actions that [you] wanted,&quot; because conspiracy actions are largely not used reactively \u2014 introducing the scene where a conspiracy action plays out is the last step of a process that generates warning signs and potentially introduces complications, but by and large a conspiracy's action budget exists as a source of Effort for its Projects (and, to a lesser degree, as a buffer against the Renegades dealing lethal damage to its Association without destroying Nodes).<br /> <br /> The conspiracy systems exist because dismantling conspiracies is one of the core gameplay loops for the PCs. As a group of mostly Baseline mortals, the meta-character of the conspiracy defines their collective abilities as an antagonistic force that is working toward goals opposed by player characters who have supernatural powers and at least some degree of alienation from human society. The system is a pacing tool and a means of constraining the threats the characters can reasonably expect to face in a given session based on their actions in the previous sessions vis-a-vis how overtly they used their powers and resources and how much pressure they've been putting on the conspiracy.<br /> <br /> Could you provide a more concrete example of where the system fell flat for you?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-10T15:32:10-07:00","id":1540147,"title":"","content":"It occurs to me that the Progenitors, the N.W.O. and ItX, all use what are basically humonculli all off which are perfectly good candidates to be deviants. All of those members of the Technocracy also heavily modify humans with little or no consideration for the results. Also great candidates to be deviants. <br /> <br /> Similarly, Pentex Fomori and psychics are also great candidates to be deviants. And when you think of it, independent Ghouls are natural allies of deviants and share a great deal with deviants in general. Risen can also be seen as a type of deviant, or at least mistaken for deviants. <br /> <br /> The crossover uses of Nodes are vast. Only Ghouls don't have multiple in built links to nodes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-10T16:12:00-07:00","id":1540151,"title":"","content":"So the number one specific mechanical thing that put me off, as best I recall:<br /> <br /> I didn't like &quot;Standing&quot; or the conspiracy attributes, I felt like I needed what this value represented to be very fluid based on the player's actions and plans. While I think it's important to have some concrete ideas about what the antagonists are capable of and allowing the player characters to learn this. As an example if the players make a poor plan to damage the conspiracy, or execute a plan poorly, or just have a trainwreck of dice rolls, then I always felt like how competent and dangerous the response by the conspiracy should be is really more about reading the room with the players and the current situation than what I wrote down before as the conspiracy attributes. Ultimately, things like what Resistance is supposed to govern kept coming down to how well I could come up with actions that I thought would be interesting/personal/evocative.<br /> This came up when one of the main antagonists (then a lynchpin of a smaller node in the conspiracy) managed to just completely defeat one of the players who was off on their own. I decided that this would be a good time to have that character be experimented on such that now they needed treatment by this antagonist at regular intervals or else they would go blind permanently. This was the smallest node in the conspiracy, but the way it played out this was about the most drastic thing I could come up with at the time. I just sort of ignored how the node was structured so that I could just do it, my players enjoyed and I loved playing that character, but it made me think, why did I bother setting up all these nodes? When the time came, I had to just ignore that system to deliver the experience I wanted to.<br /> <br /> There were other issues. I remember feeling like other things felt superfluous, or I kept having to ignore or replace things I had planned as conspiracy downtime actions and projects in response to player choices on the spot. As I said before, I kept feeling like I had to rewrite node resources and types to suit the story and player choices too. It felt pointless to actually structure a conspiracy as nodes at a certain point.<br /> <br /> Both those things combined made me wonder, why don't I just not use this system and instead just write the antagonist entirely narratively? Why track things mechanically that I really wanted to just write like a story? And so I did, and it felt much easier to run, and I feel like the game felt much more natural at that point.<br /> <br /> I don't want to say it's a bad part of the system, but it definitely didn't fit my style of game at all. I really liked how things like character options put more emphasis on flavor and experience over mechanics (and things like variations having multiple, very different explanations of how they could manifest was a wonderful example). The conspiracy mechanics felt like an entirely different side of the book, I haven't really heard anyone else talking about this part of the game. That's why I ask if other people use it seriously or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27450-alaskanemily","name":"AlaskanEmily","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-10T18:49:24-07:00","id":1540161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AlaskanEmily</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1540136-using-nodes-in-conspiracies?p=1540151#post1540151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't like &quot;Standing&quot; or the conspiracy attributes, I felt like I needed what this value represented to be very fluid based on the player's actions and plans. While I think it's important to have some concrete ideas about what the antagonists are capable of and allowing the player characters to learn this. As an example if the players make a poor plan to damage the conspiracy, or execute a plan poorly, or just have a trainwreck of dice rolls, then I always felt like how competent and dangerous the response by the conspiracy should be is really more about reading the room with the players and the current situation than what I wrote down before as the conspiracy attributes. Ultimately, things like what Resistance is supposed to govern kept coming down to how well I could come up with actions that I thought would be interesting/personal/evocative.</div> </div> </div> </div>I must confess I'm still confused on this part, because the conspiracy's Attributes only really govern how likely it is for a conspiracy action's defined stakes to be in the conspiracy's favor as opposed to including a complication that the cohort can exploit, and that's in combination with the trait that marks out the mechanical difference between &quot;this is the secret council that runs the city&quot; and &quot;this is a global megacorporation with its fingers in some extradimensional pies.&quot;<br /> <br /> They're dicepool components (and, in a Devoted chronicle, approximate limiters on the extent to which you can stretch the scope of a conspiracy action in your favor) for a pre-planned action; how competently agents on the ground deal with the PCs is handled using the normal mechanics for a scene.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This came up when one of the main antagonists (then a lynchpin of a smaller node in the conspiracy) managed to just completely defeat one of the players who was off on their own. I decided that this would be a good time to have that character be experimented on such that now they needed treatment by this antagonist at regular intervals or else they would go blind permanently. This was the smallest node in the conspiracy, but the way it played out this was about the most drastic thing I could come up with at the time. I just sort of ignored how the node was structured so that I could just do it, my players enjoyed and I loved playing that character, but it made me think, why did I bother setting up all these nodes? When the time came, I had to just ignore that system to deliver the experience I wanted to. </div> </div> </div>The mechanics of Nodes are just whether conspiracy actions originating from them optimally use Power or Finesse and what the narrative resources it provides are, and the only manner of scaled hierarchy of Nodes amount to whether they're a Hierarchical Node or not, how close to the &quot;outside&quot; of the map they are, and how many Icons, Privileges, and/or assigned Devoted are present at its location, and more than half of that relies on the optional Mapping the Conspiracy system and/or items introduced in The Devoted Companion.<br /> <br /> The only mechanics Linchpins have if you're not playing in a Devoted chronicle are that their continued loyalty and well-being are a structural weakness in the Node they maintain and that conspiracy actions led by them are more likely to succeed without complication. Otherwise they're just normal Storyteller characters of their type.<br /> <br /> I genuinely don't understand how the example relates to any sort of mechanical limitation, because the conspiracy's overall states are used for conspiracy actions and Nodes only affect those rolls in terms of trying to use a Node to do something outside of its category and that Node being infiltrated by another conspiracy. Was this being modeled as a conspiracy action in a session where the surveillance roll had failed and thus conspiracy actions directly against the cohort shouldn't be possible?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There were other issues. I remember feeling like other things felt superfluous, or I kept having to ignore or replace things I had planned as conspiracy downtime actions and projects in response to player choices on the spot. As I said before, I kept feeling like I had to rewrite node resources and types to suit the story and player choices too. It felt pointless to actually structure a conspiracy as nodes at a certain point. </div> </div> </div>I have to ask for more specifics here, because, as mentioned above, &quot;Node resources and types&quot; are very basic things like &quot;the conspiracy has people on the inside at the local police department as an Exploitative Node&quot; or &quot;the conspiracy owns the local news, which acts a Temporal Node,&quot; and Projects are largely the things the conspiracy is trying to do <i>instead of</i> mess with the cohort as part of its mission statement.<br /> <br /> Short of having a run of really bad luck with PCs taking down Nodes safeguarding Projects, I'm not sure what sort of on-the-spot changes would be needed, especially since the conspiracy creation process includes &quot;each player whose character is connected to the conspiracy should have an opportunity to define one of its Nodes (including Linchpin)&quot; as a means of flagging things that character has a ready relationship to and the kinds of problems they could be used to throw at the cohort.<br /> <br /> It's a backend tool. It exists to model what the conspiracy is doing behind the scenes and how much it can afford to be distracted from that to deal with the cohort while they try to dismantle it and/or get their lives under control, as well as how it can grow in scope and threat if left alone. You can definitely leave some Nodes undefined at the start of a game, but a conspiracy also has the resources to pursue the acquisition of new Nodes to expand its capabilities; Structural Nodes representing financial backing can support Projects in this way without needing to worry about their being inappropriate for either type of conspiracy action, or either of the other two types of non-Hierarchical Node could be used to poach resources from other conspiracies through overt or subversive means.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The conspiracy mechanics felt like an entirely different side of the book, I haven't really heard anyone else talking about this part of the game. That's why I ask if other people use it seriously or not. </div> </div> </div>They're one of my favorite parts of the game, for what it's worth, and the Devoted Companion provides a wealth of expansion and further explanation in addition to the other tools and advice it offers if you haven't checked it out already.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}